Kubernetes RBAC 

🔸RBAC = Role-Based Access Control.
Sounds complex, but it’s just about who can do what in your Kubernetes cluster.

You have two teams:
  
• Developers → Should only deploy apps and check logs.
• Operations team → Should manage the cluster, nodes, and networking.

If you give everyone cluster-admin, one wrong kubectl delete could wipe out the entire cluster 😅.

Instead, you use RBAC:
  • Create a Role for developers → Allow only create, get, list, delete on Pods & Deployments.
  • Create a ClusterRole for ops → Full access to cluster resources.
  • Bind these roles with RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding.

👉 Result: Developers can deploy apps safely. Ops can manage infra securely. Nobody steps on each other’s toes.

RBAC focuses on authorization (who can do what), while admission controllers focus on validation and mutation (what is allowed to enter the cluster)
